+
+## 21. Recalling a payout
+
+A payout that went out against the wrong numbers is taken back whole, not
+edited. `recallRewards` revokes every reward the tournament still holds and
+`distributeRewards` then runs again, minting a fresh round.
+
+Two rules keep the ledger honest. A prize somebody has claimed is gone, so a
+recall that meets one takes nothing back at all and says which rewards
+stopped it. And a round never writes over the round before it: ids carry the
+round, so an operator reading `rewards:statement` months later sees what each
+round paid and what it left standing.
+
+A statement reads the rounds back out of the reward records themselves, so it
+cannot drift from the ledger it describes. `granted` is what a round handed
+out on the day; `standing` is what is left of it, which is nothing once the
+round has been recalled. Across the whole tournament, `recalled`, `paid` and
+`outstanding` split every reward by what became of it.
+
+A balance is the same split for one player: what they have taken and what is
+still waiting. A revoked reward counts towards neither, so a recall empties a
+balance without erasing the history behind it.
+
+Claim windows close. `claimWindowMs` on the reward config is counted from the
+moment a reward was granted, the deadline itself still counts, and a claim
+after it is refused rather than silently ignored. `expireClaims` sweeps up
+afterwards: a reward past its deadline can never be claimed, so it is revoked
+rather than left sitting in somebody's balance.
